<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q1.</strong> You retrained a model and want to trust it before the arena sees it. Which command sequence is right?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="predict-next is a one-off forecast — it validates nothing about historical robustness.">paper-backtest → predict-next → arena-run live</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Train (save the bundle), validate honestly with the rolling walk-forward including shock replays, THEN screen it into the arena.">paper-backtest --save_model → walkforward-sim --stress → arena-run backtest</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="arena-run live only ticks strategies that already PASSED screening — gbm isn't in the arena until backtest screens it.">paper-backtest --save_model → arena-run live</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="rh-exec submits (dry-run) orders to Robinhood — it has no role in validation.">walkforward-sim → rh-exec → arena-run report</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q2.</strong> Your laptop was closed for four days. What do you do about the arena's missed ticks?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="There is no --backfill flag — you never need one.">Run arena-run live --backfill 4</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Nothing is corrupted; deleting the DB throws away every strategy's track record.">Delete arena_state/arena.db and re-screen</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="The live tick is self-backfilling: strategies are deterministic given data, so the next run replays the missed days exactly. Running it twice is also safe (idempotent).">Just run arena-run live — it backfills the gap automatically</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Manual SQL against the ledger defeats the deterministic-replay design and will desync equity chaining.">INSERT the missing rows into the equity table by hand</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q3.</strong> The report shows <code>meanrev: PAUSED, paused_since=2026-07-01</code>. What happened, and what's the ONLY way it trades again?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="EXCLUDED is the screening verdict; PAUSED comes from the live breaker.">It failed the backtest filter; re-run arena-run backtest</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="The drawdown breaker tripped (>20% below its running peak). Nothing auto-resumes it — you review the breaker_events row, decide, and run arena-run resume meanrev, which also resets its drawdown baseline to today.">Its live drawdown crossed the breaker threshold; arena-run resume meanrev after your review</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Divergence deliberately never pauses — it only flags.">The divergence z-test paused it; wait for z to recover</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The regime gate produces per-day holds, not a persistent status change.">The regime gate paused it; it resumes when the regime flips</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q4.</strong> Which single flag moves you from "gross-ish" results to net-of-costs results across screening?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="--trade_cost_bps tunes the LEGACY flat haircut — still turnover-blind.">--trade_cost_bps 5</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="realistic mode charges commission + half-spread + square-root impact on actual turnover; equity_gross and cost columns let you see the drag explicitly.">--cost_model realistic</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's the per-position sizing cap — risk control, not cost modeling.">--max_pos 0.30</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="fallback_adv only sets the assumed liquidity when Volume data is missing, within realistic mode.">--fallback_adv 500000</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p><strong>Q5.</strong> What does it take for real money to move on this platform?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The arena is paper-only by construction — its ledgers are SQLite rows, not brokerage orders.">A strategy passing arena-run backtest</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="predict-next prints forecasts and suggested weights; it submits nothing.">Running predict-next with --capital set</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Only rh-exec touches Robinhood, and only with the explicit --no_dry_run flag (plus RH_* credentials). Every other command in this guide is simulation or paper.">You explicitly running rh-exec --no_dry_run</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The ensemble is just another paper ledger in the arena.">Building an ensemble of PASS strategies</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<h2 id="s7">7 · Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>
<div class="scenario"><details><summary><strong>Scenario A.</strong> Monday morning routine in three commands — what would yours be?</summary>
<p>Something like: <code>arena-run live</code> (tick + backfill the weekend, watchdogs run),
<code>arena-run report</code> (read the standings, note any PAUSED/diverged rows),
and <code>predict-next --model ... --explain --check_drift</code> if you want the GBM's
view of today with its reasoning and input-health attached. <code>morning-runner</code>
can wrap the pipeline pieces you automate.</p></details></div>
<div class="scenario"><details><summary><strong>Scenario B.</strong> The report shows technical's live Sharpe at 0.1 vs backtest 1.4, divergence_z = −2.6, but it's NOT paused. Why not, and what's your move?</summary>
<p>Divergence flags but never pauses — its drawdown hasn't breached the breaker.
The z-score says live returns are inconsistent with the screening backtest beyond
sampling noise: the classic overfit signature (lesson 0036). Your move is judgment:
check <code>--check_drift</code>-style input health and the regime log; if nothing
external explains it, retire or re-screen the strategy. The system deliberately
leaves that call to you.</p></details></div>
<div class="scenario"><details><summary><strong>Scenario C.</strong> You want the engulfing strategy to trade in ALL regimes for an experiment. Two ways to do it?</summary>
<p>Quick and global: <code>--no_regime_gate</code> on the arena command (disables gating
for every strategy that run). Surgical and permanent: widen
<code>DailyEngulfingStrategy.compatible_regimes</code> in
<code>strategies/candlestick_daily.py</code> — but that's a strategy-definition change,
so re-screen afterwards.</p></details></div>

